      "narrative": {
        "summary_notes": "The Targeted Case Manager (TCM) conducted the first face-to-face contact and initial home visit at Esneldo Gomez Gomez's residence to initiate services. The TCM observed that Esneldo presented as calm, appropriately groomed, communicative, and fully engaged during the visit, with behavior consistent with a stable home-based assessment. The TCM documented that his preferred language was Spanish. Esneldo endorsed a history of depressive symptoms, anxiety, and insomnia that interfered with emotional comfort, sleep quality, stress tolerance, and independent management of ongoing healthcare responsibilities, although he reported that he was doing well with his medications at the time of the visit. Esneldo reported barriers with independently coordinating appointments, understanding and organizing healthcare paperwork, maintaining follow-through with treatment responsibilities, and arranging transportation support when needed. The TCM observed that the home was clean and organized. No structural damage, safety hazards, or environmental risks were observed inside or around the residence. The home had adequate ventilation and accessibility. Essential appliances, including a stove and refrigerator, were present and functioning properly. Electricity and hot and cold running water were available and functioning daily. No signs of physical or emotional abuse, neglect, or substance use were observed. Esneldo denied current suicidal or homicidal ideation and denied any recent safety concerns. The TCM reviewed current treatment providers, including Primary Care Provider Lazaro Salgado, APRN, 16795 NW 67TH AVE, HIALEAH, FL 330154201, phone 305-913-7300, and psychiatric provider Odania Bonilla, APRN, Healing Touch C C Inc Hialeah, 4385 West 16 Ave, Hialeah, FL 33012, phone (305) 824-0637. Esneldo reported adherence with prescribed medications and stated that the medications were beneficial. The TCM visually inspected the medication storage area and did not observe expired medications. Esneldo was independent with basic Activities of Daily Living (ADLs), including dressing, bathing, toileting, and feeding. The TCM identified needs in Instrumental Activities of Daily Living (IADLs), including coordinating appointments, organizing paperwork, understanding healthcare instructions, managing ongoing treatment responsibilities, arranging transportation, accessing resources, and handling health-related phone calls, with these difficulties associated with depression, anxiety, insomnia, and the complexity of ongoing medical and psychiatric care. The TCM explained the role and scope of Targeted Case Management services as assisting and linking clients with resources in the community to meet client needs and increase self-sufficiency and autonomy. Esneldo verbalized understanding of the information provided and agreed to participate in the Targeted Case Management program. All required admission forms were completed during the visit, and initial consents were signed to obtain Esneldo's medical and mental health information."
